# I Oppose the Compulsory Imposition of Hindi

## Professor Bharathiar's Letter

Below is a summary of the letter written by Professor S. Somasundara Bharathiar to the Education Minister on 28 June 1948 opposing the making of Hindi a compulsory subject.

"Many people have asked me whether I support the reintroduction of Hindi. They ask because some newspapers have referred to the letter I wrote to you, the Education Minister, concerning Hindi. You have not even acknowledged receiving that letter.

"I fear that the newspaper report may create the impression that I support your new policy and view regarding Hindi, which was first announced as an optional subject but has now, in practice, been made compulsory throughout the Presidency. You will agree that such an impression is directly contrary to my settled views and conclusions and to the purpose for which I wrote to you. I believe it would be better to publish the letter than to describe it in a manner that is unclear and leaves room for doubt.

"On 6 June 1948, I wrote commending you for boldly and wisely declaring that, although Hindi had been made compulsory in the Presidency's other linguistic regions, it would remain optional in Tamil Nadu.

"You know that I oppose the compulsory imposition of Hindi, but I do not oppose its propagation as a voluntarily accepted national language in the new India.

"Because the public announcement contained no indication that Hindi would be made compulsory, I said that the people of Tamil Nadu should welcome your liberal policy. But the compulsory burden of three languages now placed upon the young people in every school in this Presidency reveals the course of the Congress ministry even in matters of educational policy.

"Nowhere else in all India have three languages been imposed compulsorily in this manner."

(*Dravida Nadu*, 47.48)
